+        # The stock schedule starts decaying at step 50,000 and steps again only
+        # every 50,000 after that, which suits a run far longer than this one: at
+        # a 51,200-step budget it yields a single 5% step in the last 1,200 steps,
+        # so the model trains at the constant 1e-3 plateau throughout. Left alone
+        # that is not an anneal, and with a global batch of 8 -- a sixteenth of
+        # the batch the 1e-3 was chosen for -- the model wanders rather than
+        # settles.
+        #
+        # Resolved in three layers: upstream defaults, then a JSON file in the run
+        # root, then the environment. An unset environment and no file reproduce
+        # stock behaviour exactly.
+        #
+        # The file layer exists because the environment alone cannot reach a run
+        # already in flight. Slurm requeues the batch script as it was stored at
+        # submission, so exports added to that script afterwards never arrive --
+        # a schedule declared there would apply only to a lineage submitted after
+        # the edit, and this run's lineage began in September and requeues every
+        # three days. Cancelling to pick up the new script would surrender an H100
+        # allocation that took hours to win. The run root is read fresh by every
+        # process at startup, belongs to exactly one run, and sits beside the
+        # checkpoints it governs, so the schedule travels with the experiment
+        # rather than with the job script.
+        lr_kwargs = dict(
+            max_lr=1e-3,
+            warmup_no_steps=1000,
+            start_decay_after_n_steps=50000,
+            decay_every_n_steps=50000,
+            decay_factor=0.95,
